nodecode-payops is a rewrite of BigWave45's BigWave45/payops-hermes-:

  https://github.com/BigWave45/payops-hermes-
  commit 7712f084392769356bdb294c4b48bf99bf7faadb
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 PayOps contributors (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the two model-facing tools and their argument schemas
(payops_list_transactions, payops_reconcile), the one host they reach
(api.paystack.co), the normalization of Paystack's transaction and
settlement rows into provider-neutral records, the paged read with its
per-call page cap and the warning it leaves when the cap cuts a range
short, and the deterministic reconciliation report — per-currency
summaries on both sides (successful and failed counts, successful
amounts, settled and not-final settlements), duplicate references
detected within one provider, and the original's own statement of what
the report does not claim. The Python (urllib, dataclasses) is Common
Lisp here: nlk:http is the one seam, one section declaration carries the
settings, and two nlk:define-contract declarations carry the tools'
arguments, so the schema the model is shown and the body that runs are
one shape. The original's currency tables in money.py are kept whole, and
drive the amount formatting the /payops command uses.

Left out: the Flutterwave v4 development line, which the original's own
README excludes from its catalog release because it needed an
OAuth grant the harness did not support; the secret read through the
harness's secret route, which here is the environment variable the
section names (or a key written in the section), so a cell needs no host
API; the no-redirect opener and the 5 MB response cap, replaced by one
base_url that must be https and a per-call page cap, since nlk:http owns
the dial; the Python packaging (pyproject.toml, tests, CI workflows),
which a cell's folder and its test system already are; and the
provider-errors list, which now carries nothing because a failed call
answers its own refusal in words instead of an empty record.
